add(270,58,['20–67'],[(58,'1–2023')],'other','[Source provenance, not a manuscript assertion.]','The only current source endpoints for these notes are the two supplied Pro transcription packages and their notes/doubts. BT, BN, BD, Hua, RESULTS_STATE, context-search records and original scan images named by the earlier run are not included in this task.','Claims that an earlier run read such material are retained as provenance statements; this run cannot call those unavailable endpoints checked.','overstates','The source-key and closing-source sections describe what an earlier run received, not what is supplied to this consolidation.',[s58(1,'corrected','Recast all context-ZIP/project/source claims as earlier-run provenance; only the current transcription is an inspected endpoint.'),s58(85,'merged','The closing manuscript/project bibliography repeats the same unavailable-source provenance and is merged here.')])
add(271,58,[22,24,26],[(58,'70–113,152–193,238–288')],'other','Ass_B(E⊗_A F)','The reused primary-decomposition typescript states the associated-prime formula for scalar extension through an A-flat B-module F; equality is asserted when A is noetherian, with a fibrewise form under noetherian A and B.','The sequence runs in reverse scan order26→24→22. It is an ancillary algebra text, not a Picard theorem.','agrees','N01 correctly isolates the flatness hypothesis and reversed textual sequence.',[n58(1,'kept','Keep the algebraic statement and its flatness condition; no Picard conclusion follows merely from its location.')])
add(272,58,[21],[(58,'38–69')],'unproved claim','connexe en dimension ≥h','A local-to-global connectedness-in-dimension lemma is proposed for connected noetherian X whose irreducible components have dimension at least h, using punctured local spectra at closed points and a stronger marginal condition at points of small closure dimension.','The k/h bound is uncertain and the written proof stops before its contradiction is completed.','agrees','N02 locates both the statement and incomplete proof, without importing a stronger modern formulation.',[n58(2,'kept','The dimension letter and marginal strengthening stay separate; the proof is not called complete.')])
add(273,58,[23,25,27],[(58,'114–151,194–237,289–323')],'reading question','connexe','The live theorem says the generic fibre is connected in the relevant dimension. A modernization that says geometrically connected adds a substantive hypothesis rather than resolving the already legible word connexe.','The stronger geometric statement may be true under further hypotheses, but it is not substituted into the diplomatic text.','agrees','N03 correctly distinguishes mathematical repair from ink.',[n58(3,'kept','Keep the geometric qualifier as a proposed hypothesis repair, not as a recovered reading.')])
add(274,58,[23],[(58,'114–151')],'unproved claim','X_1 connexe','The literal specialization theorem lacks geometric connectedness of the generic fibre. Let A=Z_(5), B=A[i], X=P¹_B over Spec A, and h=0. The generic fibre is connected of dimension1, the base is regular and geometrically unibranch, X is proper and its only irreducible component dominates; the special fibre is two disjoint projective lines.','This independently verified counterexample refutes only the literal formulation; it does not refute a geometric-generic-fibre repair.','agrees','N04 checks every written hypothesis rather than relying on an analogy.',[n58(4,'kept','The split special fibre follows from T²+1=(T−2)(T+2) over F5, while the generic quadratic algebra is a field.')])
add(275,58,[25],[(58,'194–237')],'conjecture','facile (?)','The reduction in Lemma5 invokes formal equidimensionality of local rings in the relevant dominant family and labels this step uncertain.','Excellence, universal catenarity and dimension-theorem hypotheses can supply familiar cases; an arbitrary noetherian assertion is not silently accepted.','agrees','N05 retains the author’s question and the formal-local character of the needed input.',[n58(5,'kept','The completion/equidimensionality step remains a distinct problem, not a proved intermediate lemma.')])
add(276,58,[27],[(58,'289–306')],'unproved claim','K+1=(n+h)+1','Cutting by n equations is claimed to lower connectedness dimension by at most n, with K=n+h in the displayed reduction.','The numerical shift is checked independently; it does not validate the preceding specialization or formal-equidimensionality assumptions.','agrees','N06 correctly limits what the arithmetic check establishes.',[n58(6,'kept','Keep the verified K−n=h calculation separate from the theorem’s geometric premises.')])
add(277,58,[27],[(58,'299–323')],'conjecture','connexité géométrique','The page proposes variants with geometrically connected fibres, proper universally open maps and intersections with hyperplanes. These have distinct hypotheses and dimension bounds.','A connectedness theorem for ample intersections is not an unconditional specialization theorem for a merely connected generic fibre.','agrees','N07 correctly separates the variants.',[n58(7,'kept','Do not use a later hyperplane theorem to certify all the specialization variants.')])
add(278,58,[27],[(58,'307–323')],'self-flagged step','généraliser','The author suggests a version for a general morphism to projective space and a local analogue of the hyperplane result.','The inverse-image and local formulations require their own positivity/dimension or completion assumptions.','agrees','N08 preserves two proposed extensions rather than treating them as consequences already proved.',[n58(8,'kept','Keep both generalizations in the problem index with separate modern matches.')])
add(279,58,[30,31],[(58,'334–443')],'conjecture','généraliserons','The introduction proposes extending proper formal comparison and existence to selected nonproper schemes using depth and cohomological finiteness; the range is advertised as cohomology up to a depth-minus-two bound.','This is a programme and advertised theorem range; the heavily revised introductory formulas do not define a single complete statement.','agrees','N09 recognizes the programme’s scope without identifying all formal objects prematurely.',[n58(9,'kept','Preserve comparison, finiteness and algebraization as distinct goals.')])
